fix(reconciler): count a write outside every served repository as uncheckable (#169)

RM-104's pass corroborates a reported write against the repository, because
`record_event`'s `payload_digest` is validated for shape and compared to
nothing. It counted a claim naming a path in NO repository it can read as
"not found" — which says the repository contradicted the claim when the
repository was never asked, and makes the corroboration rate move with how
much scratch work an agent did rather than with anything about integrity.

Those claims are now Uncheckable, the bucket that already meant "this check
is silent about it". Deciding it needs the host-to-mount translation
`describe_workspace` performs, for that tool's reason: a body records the
path the HARNESS saw, and nothing in this process says what its own mount
corresponds to. Nothing is guessed — a deployment that has not been told
translates nothing, admits every path, and the cycle report says UNSCOPED so
the weaker number is never read as the stronger one.

Measured with the shipped code over this deployment's retained bodies and its
own served list: 37,880 bodies, 4,291 reconstructable claims, 339 (7.9%) now
uncheckable. #169 projected 75.8% from a DIFFERENT population — that figure
is over every body carrying a `file_path`, and 2,895 of those 7,183 are
`Read`, which ClaimFromBody already discards (REC-014). The rule is right;
its effect on the rate is single digits, not four in five.

A gitignored path inside a repository is still counted "not found": this rule
asks where the write was, not whether git was ever going to track it.

Driven by TestWritesPassCountsAWriteOutsideEveryServedRepositoryAsUncheckable,
watched failing at "uncheckable 0, want 1" and "unsupported 2, want 1", and by
TestWritesPassSaysWhenItCannotScopeAPath, which holds the unscoped report
open.

